== A Change of Nature

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[VII. Another thing in which gracious affections are distinguished from others is that #emph[they are attended with a change of nature.]]

All gracious affections arise from a spiritual understanding, in which the excellence and glory of divine things are shown to the soul, as was shown before. But all spiritual manifestations are transforming. They do not only alter the present exercise, sensation and frame of the soul; they have such power and efficacy that they alter the very nature of the soul. 2 Cor 3:18: “And we all, with unveiled faces, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into the same image from glory to glory, even as by the Spirit of the Lord.” Power like this is properly divine power, and belongs to #emph[the Spirit of the Lord] alone. Other power may make a great alteration in men’s present frames and feelings, but only the power of a Creator can change the nature, or give a new nature. And no manifestations or illuminations will have this supernatural effect except those that are divine and supernatural. But all manifestations that are truly divine do have this effect. The soul is deeply affected by these manifestations, and so affected as to be transformed.

So it is with the affections the soul has in its conversion. The ways Scripture represents conversion strongly imply and signify a change of nature: #emph[being born again; becoming new creatures; rising from the dead; being renewed in the spirit of the mind; dying to sin, and living to righteousness; putting off the old man, and putting on the new man; being grafted into a new stock; having a divine seed implanted in the heart; being made partakers of the divine nature,] and so on.

Therefore, if there is no great, remarkable and abiding change in persons who think they have experienced a work of conversion, all their imaginations and claims are vain, however they have been affected. #footnote[I would not judge of the whole soul’s coming to Christ so much by sudden pangs as by an inward bent. For the whole soul may be carried to Christ in affectionate expressions and actions; but without this bent, and change of affections, it is unsound. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 203.] Conversion (if we may give any credit to the Scripture) is a great and universal change of the man, turning him from sin to God. A man may be restrained from sin before he is converted. But when he is converted, he is not only restrained from sin; his very heart and nature are turned from it to holiness, so that from then on he becomes a holy person and an enemy to sin. Suppose, then, that after a person’s high affections at his supposed first conversion, it comes in a little while to this: there is no very perceptible or remarkable alteration in him as to the bad qualities and evil habits that were visible in him before. He is ordinarily under the sway of the same kind of dispositions he used to have, and the same things seem to belong to his character. He appears as selfish, as carnal, as dull and perverse, as unchristian and unsavory as ever. Then this is greater evidence against him than the brightest story of experiences that was ever told is for him. For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision, neither high profession nor low profession, neither a fair story nor a broken one, counts for anything, but a new creature.

If a very great alteration is visible in a person for a while, but it does not last, and afterward he returns, in a settled way, to be much as he used to be, it appears to be no change of nature. For nature is an abiding thing. A pig, which is of a filthy nature, may be washed, but its swinish nature remains. And a dove, which is of a clean nature, may be soiled, but its clean nature remains. #footnote[It is with the soul as with water: all the cold may be gone, but the native principle of cold remains still. You may remove the burning of lusts, but not the blackness of nature. Where the power of sin lies, there may be a change of conscience from complacency to terror, a change of life from profaneness to outward respectability and the fashions of the world, to escape its pollutions, a change of lusts, indeed a quenching of them for a time; but the nature is never changed, in the best hypocrite that ever was. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, Part I. P. 194.]

Allowances must indeed be made for the natural temperament. Conversion does not entirely root out the natural temperament. The sins a man was most inclined to by his natural constitution before his conversion, he may still be most apt to fall into. But conversion will still make a great alteration even with respect to these sins. Though grace, while it is imperfect, does not root out an evil natural temperament, it has great power and efficacy to correct it. The change worked in conversion is a universal change. Grace changes a man with respect to whatever is sinful in him. The #emph[old man] is put off and the #emph[new man] put on. They are sanctified throughout, and the man becomes a new creature: old things have passed away, and #emph[all things] have become new. All sin is mortified, sins of constitution as well as others. Suppose that before his conversion a man was especially inclined by his natural constitution to lewdness, or drunkenness, or malice. Converting grace will make a great alteration in him with respect to these evil dispositions. However much he may still be most in danger of these sins, they will no longer have dominion over him, nor will they properly be his character any more. Indeed, true repentance in some respects turns a man especially against his own iniquity: #emph[that] in which he has been most guilty and has chiefly dishonored God. The man who forsakes other sins but spares his leading sin, the iniquity he is chiefly inclined to, is like #emph[Saul] when he was sent against God’s enemies the #emph[Amalekites.] He had a strict charge to spare none of them alive but to destroy them utterly, small and great. He utterly destroyed the common people, but spared the king, the chief of them all, alive.

Some foolishly make it an argument in favor of their manifestations and affections that, when these are gone, they are left wholly without any life or sense, or anything beyond what they had before. They think it shows that what they experienced was wholly of God and not of themselves, because (they say) when God has departed, all is gone; they can see and feel nothing, and are no better than they used to be.

It is very true that all grace and goodness in the hearts of the saints is entirely from God, and they depend on him for it universally and immediately. But these persons are still mistaken about the way in which God imparts himself and his Holy Spirit when he imparts saving grace to the soul. He gives his Spirit to be united to the faculties of the soul, and to dwell there in the manner of a principle of nature. So the soul, in being endowed with grace, is endowed with a new nature; and nature is an abiding thing. All the exercises of grace are entirely from Christ. But those exercises are not from Christ in the way that something alive moves and stirs something that is without life, which still remains without life. They are from him as having life imparted to the soul, so that through Christ’s power it has a vital nature inherent in itself. In the soul where Christ savingly is, there he lives. He does not only live #emph[outside] it, so as to move it by force; he lives in it, so that it too is alive. Grace in the soul is as much from Christ as the light in a glass held out in the sunbeams is from the sun. But this pictures the way grace is imparted to the soul only in part. For the glass remains as it was; its nature is not changed at all, and it is as much without any light in its own nature as ever. But the soul of a saint receives light from the Sun of righteousness in such a way that its nature is changed, and it properly becomes a luminous thing. Not only does the sun shine in the saints; they also become little suns, partaking of the nature of the fountain of their light. In this respect the way they receive their light is like that of the lamps in the tabernacle rather than that of a reflecting glass. Though those lamps were lit by fire from heaven, they thereby became burning, shining things themselves. The saints do not only drink of the water of life that flows from the original fountain; this water becomes a fountain of water in them, springing up there and flowing out of them (John 4:14; and 7:38, 39). Grace is compared to a seed implanted, which is not only in the ground but has hold of it, has root there and grows there, and is an abiding principle of life and nature there.

As it is with the spiritual manifestations and affections given at first conversion, so it is with all illuminations and affections of that kind that persons have afterward: they are all #emph[transforming.] There is a divine power and energy in them like that in the first manifestations. They still reach the bottom of the heart, and affect and alter the very nature of the soul, in proportion to the degree in which they are given. And by them a transformation of nature is continued and carried on to the end of life, until it is brought to perfection in glory. That is why Scripture represents the progress of the work of grace in the hearts of the saints as a continued conversion and renewal of nature. So the apostle exhorts those at #emph[Rome, loved by God and called to be saints,] who had received God’s redeeming #emph[mercies,] to be #emph[transformed by the renewing of their mind.] Rom 12:1, 2: “Therefore I urge you, brothers, by the mercies of God, to offer your bodies as living sacrifices … Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind.” Compare chapter 1:7. So too the apostle writes to the #emph[saints and faithful in Christ Jesus] who were at #emph[Ephesus] (Eph 1:1). These were people who #emph[were once dead in trespasses and sins,] but had now been #emph[made alive, raised up, and seated together in the heavenly realms in Christ, created in Christ Jesus for good works. They were once far away but had now been brought near by the blood of Christ. They were no longer strangers and foreigners, but fellow citizens with the saints and members of God’s household, built together into a dwelling place for God through the Spirit.] Writing to these people, I say, the apostle tells them #emph[that he did not stop praying for them, that God would give them a spirit of wisdom and revelation in the knowledge of Christ, the eyes of their understanding being enlightened, so that they might know,] or experience, #emph[the surpassing greatness of God’s power toward those who believe, in accordance with the working of his mighty power, which he exerted in Christ when he raised him from the dead and seated him at his own right hand in the heavenly realms] (Eph 1:16 to the end). In this the apostle has in view the glorious power and work of God in converting and renewing the soul, as is very plain from what follows. So the apostle exhorts the same persons #emph[to put off the old man, which is corrupt according to the deceitful lusts, and to be renewed in the spirit of their minds, and to put on the new man, which is created after God in righteousness and true holiness] (Eph 4:22, 23, 24).

There is a sort of high affections that some have from time to time which leave them without any appearance at all of an abiding effect. They go off suddenly, so that from the very height of their emotion and seeming rapture they pass at once to being quite dead, empty of all sense and activity. Surely it is not usually so with high gracious affections. #footnote[Do you think the Holy Spirit comes on a man, as on #emph[Balaam,] by an immediate acting, and then leaves him, and then he has nothing? #emph[Shepard]’s Par. Part I. P. 126.] They leave a sweet savor and relish of divine things on the heart, and a stronger bent of soul toward God and holiness. So #emph[Moses]’s face not only shone while he was on the mountain, conversing with God in an extraordinary way; it went on shining after he came down from the mountain. When men have been conversing with Christ in an extraordinary way, a perceptible effect of it remains on them. There is something remarkable in their disposition and frame; and if we take note of it and trace it to its cause, we will find it is because they have been with Jesus (Acts 4:13).
